<https://example.org/wikigraph/snapshot/wikipedia/29414838/1375530180> <https://example.org/wikigraph/text> "General-purpose programming language Rust Paradigms Multi-paradigm : concurrent functional generic imperative structured Developer The Rust Team First appeared January 20, 2012 ; 14 years ago ( 2012-01-20 ) Stable release 1.98.1 / September 3, 2026 ; 15 days ago ( September 3, 2026 ) Typing discipline Affine inferred nominal static strong Implementation language OCaml (2006–2011) Rust (2012–present) Platform Cross-platform OS Cross-platform License MIT , Apache 2.0 Filename extensions .rs , .rlib Website rust-lang.org Influenced by Alef BETA CLU C# C++ Cyclone Elm Erlang Haskell Hermes Limbo Mesa Napier Newsqueak NIL OCaml Ruby Sather Scheme Standard ML Swift Influenced Idris PHP Project Verona SPARK Swift V Zig Gleam Rust is a general-purpose programming language that emphasizes performance , type safety , concurrency , and memory safety . Rust supports multiple programming paradigms . It was influenced by ideas from functional programming , including immutability , higher-order functions , algebraic data types , and pattern matching . It also supports object-oriented programming via structs, enums , traits, and methods. Rust enforces memory safety (i.e., that all references point to valid memory) without a conventional garbage collector ; instead, memory safety errors and data races are prevented by the \"borrow checker\", which tracks the object lifetime of references at compile time . Software developer Graydon Hoare created Rust in 2006 while working at Mozilla , which officially sponsored the project in 2009. The first stable release, Rust 1.0, was published in May 2015. The project has been sponsored by the Rust Foundation since February 2021. Rust has been adopted by many software projects, especially web services and system software . It has been studied academically and has a growing community of developers. ^ Anderson, Brian (2012-01-24). \"[rust-dev] The Rust compiler 0.1 is unleashed\" . rust-dev (Mailing list). Archived from the original on 2012-01-24 . Retrieved 2025-01-07 . ^ \"Announcing Rust 1.98.1\" . 2026-09-03 . Retrieved 2026-09-04 . ^ a b \"Platform Support\" . The rustc book . Archived from the original on 2022-06-30 . Retrieved 2022-06-27 . ^ \"Copyright\" . GitHub . The Rust Programming Language. 2022-10-19. Archived from the original on 2023-07-22 . Retrieved 2022-10-19 . ^ Cite error: The named reference licenses was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Strom, Robert E. (1983). \"Mechanisms for compile-time enforcement of security\". Proceedings of the 10th ACM SIGACT-SIGPLAN symposium on Principles of programming languages - POPL '83 . pp. 276– 284. doi : 10.1145/567067.567093 . ISBN 0897910907 . S2CID 6630704 . ^ Strom, Robert E.; Yemini, Shaula (1986). \"Typestate: A programming language concept for enhancing software reliability\" (PDF) . IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering . 12 (1). IEEE: 157– 171. Bibcode : 1986ITSEn..12..157S . doi : 10.1109/tse.1986.6312929 . S2CID 15575346 . ^ \"Uniqueness Types\" . Rust Blog . Archived from the original on 2016-09-15 . Retrieved 2016-10-08 . Those of you familiar with the Elm style may recognize that the updated --explain messages draw heavy inspiration from the Elm approach. ^ Cite error: The named reference influences was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ \"Uniqueness Types\" . Idris 1.3.3 documentation . Archived from the original on 2018-11-21 . Retrieved 2022-07-14 . They are inspired by ... ownership types and borrowed pointers in the Rust programming language. ^ Eberlei, Benjamin; Schröder, Martin (2020-03-09). \"PHP RFC: Attributes v2\" . wiki.php.net . Retrieved 2026-05-11 . ^ Tovilo, Ilija (2020-05-22). \"PHP RFC: Match expression v2\" . wiki.php.net . Retrieved 2020-11-29 . ^ Garfield, Larry; Tovilo, Ilija (2020-12-04). \"PHP RFC: Enumerations\" . wiki.php.net . Retrieved 2026-05-11 . ^ Garfield, Larry; Tovilo, Ilija (2020-11-11). \"PHP RFC: Pattern Matching\" . wiki.php.net . Retrieved 2026-05-11 . ^ Cite error: The named reference Project Verona was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Cite error: The named reference Jaloyan was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ Cite error: The named reference Lattner was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). ^ \"V documentation (Introduction)\" . GitHub . The V Programming Language . Retrieved 2023-11-04 . ^ Yegulalp, Serdar (2016-08-29). \"New challenger joins Rust to topple C language\" . InfoWorld . Archived from the original on 2021-11-25 . Retrieved 2022-10-19 . ^ Cite error: The named reference Gleam was invoked but never defined (see the help page ). Cite error: There are <ref group=note> tags on this page, but the references will not show without a {{reflist|group=note}} template (see the help page )." .
